Before mounting S3 bucket, we need to create a new role and give permission to the EC2 for S3 access

After the policy is created, select EC2 > Actions > Security > Modify IAM Role > Choose the Role created.


// On Ubuntu/Debian
sudo apt update
sudo apt install s3fs

// On CentOS/RHEL
sudo yum install s3fs-fuse

// Create a Mount Point: Choose or create a directory where you want to mount the S3 bucket.
mkdir ~/my-s3-bucket

// Mount the S3 Bucket: Use s3fs to mount the bucket.

sudo s3fs my-bucket-name ~/my-s3-bucket -o iam_role=auto -o  allow_other

// Adjust Permissions
// To ensure that you have read and write access:

// Set the Correct Permissions: You might want to change ownership or set permissions on the mount point:
sudo chown -R $(whoami):$(whoami) ~/my-s3-bucket

// Unmounting: When you’re done, you can unmount the S3 bucket with:
fusermount -u ~/my-s3-bucket


Troubleshooting
If you encounter permission issues, check the IAM policies associated with your AWS credentials to ensure they have the necessary permissions for S3 operations.
Ensure that the allow_other option is supported by your system's FUSE configuration.
With this setup, you should have read and write access to your S3 bucket through the mounted local folder. 

